01:15:43.170 --> 01:15:44.740
that if you're having a bad day,

1820
01:15:44.740 --> 01:15:45.830
so when I'm having a bad day,

1821
01:15:45.830 --> 01:15:48.300
I put on my pajamas, get back in bed.

1822
01:15:48.300 --> 01:15:50.490
Or if I'm somewhere else, I just pretend

1823
01:15:50.490 --> 01:15:52.780
and just lay and close my eyes for one minute

1824
01:15:52.780 --> 01:15:54.850
and then jump out of bed and yell new day

1825
01:15:54.850 --> 01:15:57.850
and now I'm allowed to have a new day.

1826
01:15:57.850 --> 01:15:58.890
I love it.
That's a great one.

1827
01:15:58.890 --> 01:16:00.539
I know, that's brilliant.

1828
01:16:00.539 --> 01:16:02.847
Especially all of us working from home.

1829
01:16:04.781 --> 01:16:09.023
And you could do this at like 10:00 a.m. or at 5:00 p.m.

1830
01:16:09.023 --> 01:16:11.970
And like you're allowed to imagine that this is a new day.

1831
01:16:11.970 --> 01:16:14.340
Like what's so arbitrary, the sky is out.

1832
01:16:14.340 --> 01:16:16.057
You know what I mean?

1833
01:16:16.057 --> 01:16:18.333
The moon isn't the right place.

1834
01:16:18.333 --> 01:16:20.350
Just imagine it's a new day.

1835
01:16:20.350 --> 01:16:22.440
Like we have to use our imagination here

1836
01:16:22.440 --> 01:16:23.640
and not see everything

1837
01:16:23.640 --> 01:16:25.820
as a reason why we're not gonna get to our goals.

1838
01:16:25.820 --> 01:16:27.850
But as a reason why we are anyway.

1839
01:16:27.850 --> 01:16:29.793
And it's back to the fund aspect

1840
01:16:29.793 --> 01:16:31.350
that Rich was bringing up.

1841
01:16:31.350 --> 01:16:32.183
Enjoy.

1842
01:16:33.810 --> 01:16:35.870
This is supposed to be fun.

1843
01:16:35.870 --> 01:16:37.700
Which a lot of people struggle with.

1844
01:16:37.700 --> 01:16:39.540
Especially the kick out of the new year

1845
01:16:39.540 --> 01:16:43.180
and they have this expectation for themselves

1846
01:16:43.180 --> 01:16:45.020
and they quickly lose that fun.

1847
01:16:45.020 --> 01:16:46.670
And then it becomes a vicious cycle

1848
01:16:46.670 --> 01:16:49.730
of just beating yourself up and it's almost worse

1849
01:16:49.730 --> 01:16:51.050
'cause then you feel like, well,

1850
01:16:51.050 --> 01:16:52.440
I tried and I couldn't do it.

1851
01:16:52.440 --> 01:16:56.360
And I'm proving that story that I knew deep down was true

1852
01:16:56.360 --> 01:16:59.210
that I suck and I can't do hard things.

1853
01:16:59.210 --> 01:17:01.400
It's like, you always wanna prove yourself

1854
01:17:01.400 --> 01:17:04.020
but maybe this year we shall prove ourselves wrong.

1855
01:17:04.020 --> 01:17:06.303
Yeah, you have to prove that you're year-

1856
01:17:06.303 --> 01:17:08.003
Because you're basically saying,

1857
01:17:08.890 --> 01:17:10.760
we are inherently hard on ourselves

1858
01:17:10.760 --> 01:17:12.400
and our default setting is to be like,

1859
01:17:12.400 --> 01:17:16.050
I'm not, I'm not, I'm not, I'm not capable or this and that.

1860
01:17:16.050 --> 01:17:18.270
So maybe instead of our heart,

1861
01:17:18.270 --> 01:17:19.740
'cause the heart always wants to be right.

1862
01:17:19.740 --> 01:17:20.573
So you always wanna be like,

1863
01:17:20.573 --> 01:17:23.910
see, I will get rejected or see, I will fail.

1864
01:17:23.910 --> 01:17:28.120
And maybe like, we can allow ourselves to be wrong.

1865
01:17:28.120 --> 01:17:31.230
And that is really hard for high achieving athletes

1866
01:17:31.230 --> 01:17:32.930
because we wanna be perfect.

1867
01:17:32.930 --> 01:17:34.400
And part of being perfect

1868
01:17:34.400 --> 01:17:36.340
is having the thing we feel in our heart,

1869
01:17:36.340 --> 01:17:38.950
which might be an adequacy be true.

1870
01:17:38.950 --> 01:17:41.930
And I feel like something that I'm trying to do

1871
01:17:41.930 --> 01:17:45.260
is understand that I'm wrong a lot of the times,

1872
01:17:45.260 --> 01:17:47.240
like I mess up and just last week

1873
01:17:47.240 --> 01:17:49.150
I got in a fight with my friend.

1874
01:17:49.150 --> 01:17:51.740
I feel so bad 'cause I'm so much older

1875
01:17:51.740 --> 01:17:54.420
than someone who's got a fight with a friend.

1876
01:17:54.420 --> 01:17:56.660
And I was like, felt so bad.

1877
01:17:56.660 --> 01:18:00.240
And I apologized and I feel like I said, sorry,

1878
01:18:00.240 --> 01:18:01.240
and all this stuff.

1879
01:18:01.240 --> 01:18:03.930
And I wasn't very good at that growing up

1880
01:18:03.930 --> 01:18:06.430
because we're so stubborn and we just wanna be right.

1881
01:18:06.430 --> 01:18:08.260
And I was like, I was wrong.

1882
01:18:08.260 --> 01:18:12.020
And it freed me because he accepted my apology

1883
01:18:12.020 --> 01:18:13.210
and we're totally fine.

1884
01:18:13.210 --> 01:18:16.446
And it proved to me that I could be wrong and still survive.

1885
01:18:16.446 --> 01:18:21.050
And I think, I don't know why I got to this point,

1886
01:18:21.050 --> 01:18:23.120
but like, we're wrong a lot of the time

1887
01:18:23.120 --> 01:18:25.880
so we shouldn't think that the thing we fear most inside

1888
01:18:25.880 --> 01:18:27.540
is true.

1889
01:18:27.540 --> 01:18:29.120
I think that's right.

1890
01:18:29.120 --> 01:18:33.770
And I think a lot of it is there's a lot to be learned

1891
01:18:33.770 --> 01:18:37.510
from trying to return to a more childlike state

1892
01:18:37.510 --> 01:18:38.343
in all of this.

1893
01:18:38.343 --> 01:18:41.090
Because as kids, we're naturally curious,

1894
01:18:41.090 --> 01:18:46.080
we wanna have adventures, we're not afraid.

1895
01:18:46.080 --> 01:18:46.970
We're not hard on ourselves.

1896
01:18:46.970 --> 01:18:49.330
Yeah, we're not hard on ourselves,

1897
01:18:49.330 --> 01:18:52.300
like fear, insecurity, defeatism

1898
01:18:52.300 --> 01:18:54.120
all of these things are learned behaviors

1899
01:18:54.120 --> 01:18:55.920
that wear us down over time

1900
01:18:55.920 --> 01:18:58.170
and distance ourselves from like

1901
01:18:58.170 --> 01:19:03.010
that kind of pure, beautiful nature that still is within us,

1902
01:19:03.010 --> 01:19:05.170
but needs a little tarnishing.

1903
01:19:05.170 --> 01:19:07.330
And I think the more that we can connect with that,

1904
01:19:07.330 --> 01:19:09.430
whatever goal you set for yourself,

1905
01:19:09.430 --> 01:19:13.580
it allows you to kind of pursue it with more of that,

1906
01:19:13.580 --> 01:19:17.380
like joy and curiosity and imagination.

1907
01:19:17.380 --> 01:19:18.870
Like wild flowers.
It's interesting

1908
01:19:18.870 --> 01:19:21.180
you say that because you know,

1909
01:19:21.180 --> 01:19:23.880
a lot of athletes at this time of year,

1910
01:19:23.880 --> 01:19:28.720
you're going into the new year already at your lowest mood.

1911
01:19:28.720 --> 01:19:32.610
It's dark, it's hard time of year the holidays,

1912
01:19:32.610 --> 01:19:34.010
but also there's no races.

1913
01:19:34.010 --> 01:19:35.570
I know a lot of my athletes,

1914
01:19:35.570 --> 01:19:38.100
they struggle at this time of year with motivation.

1915
01:19:38.100 --> 01:19:41.512
And so then you're supposed to create this new thing

1916
01:19:41.512 --> 01:19:43.387
just cause the day changes.

1917
01:19:43.387 --> 01:19:45.710
The days aren't so-
Be all excited about it.

1918
01:19:45.710 --> 01:19:46.543
And be all excited.

1919
01:19:46.543 --> 01:19:48.530
So you're already coming in the low energy

1920
01:19:48.530 --> 01:19:51.037
and you're sort of trying to pump yourself up.

1921
01:19:51.037 --> 01:19:54.230
And I think that ties into why a lot of people

1922
01:19:54.230 --> 01:19:55.590
struggle with following through

1923
01:19:55.590 --> 01:19:58.360
'cause you already came in with like, ugh,

1924
01:19:58.360 --> 01:20:00.320
the holiday holidays are wearing me down,

1925
01:20:00.320 --> 01:20:04.245
I'm stressed out, I'm tired, I'm exhausted, it's dark,

1926
01:20:04.245 --> 01:20:06.350
there's no sun.

1927
01:20:06.350 --> 01:20:09.190
All the things I like to do or I'm not able to do.

1928
01:20:09.190 --> 01:20:12.200
And there's been no events so I don't have that high from,

1929
01:20:12.200 --> 01:20:14.760
training for something and so you come into it.

1930
01:20:14.760 --> 01:20:17.090
So it's a hard time of year already

1931
01:20:17.090 --> 01:20:20.870
and it turns into this like laborious exercise of like,

1932
01:20:20.870 --> 01:20:22.320
all right, who do I wanna be?

1933
01:20:24.000 --> 01:20:25.593
On top of all the exhaustion.

1934
01:20:25.593 --> 01:20:26.426
Exactly.

1935
01:20:26.426 --> 01:20:28.130
And that takes the fun and the imagination

1936
01:20:28.130 --> 01:20:30.100
and the childlike state out of any of it.

1937
01:20:30.100 --> 01:20:31.880
It's so far removed.

1938
01:20:31.880 --> 01:20:36.470
So it's figuring out like how do I emerge into spring

1939
01:20:36.470 --> 01:20:39.750
with maybe some energy to do the things that I wanna do

1940
01:20:39.750 --> 01:20:41.660
in the early summer or summer

1941
01:20:41.660 --> 01:20:43.600
and I'm not starting from scratch.

1942
01:20:43.600 --> 01:20:46.780
Like even just little steps like that saying, okay,

1943
01:20:46.780 --> 01:20:50.130
how do I wanna come out of hibernation?

1944
01:20:50.130 --> 01:20:51.870
Forgive myself for now.

1945
01:20:51.870 --> 01:20:53.120
I don't wanna train right now.

1946
01:20:53.120 --> 01:20:54.480
I don't wanna work out right now.

1947
01:20:54.480 --> 01:20:56.170
I don't wanna do a lot of stuff.

1948
01:20:56.170 --> 01:20:59.310
I'm just low energy that's okay.

1949
01:20:59.310 --> 01:21:01.640
But let's take a longer term approach.

1950
01:21:01.640 --> 01:21:03.410
Like who do I wanna be on April ones?

1951
01:21:03.410 --> 01:21:05.890
So what can I do today gently

1952
01:21:05.890 --> 01:21:08.310
that'll help me prepare for April one,

1953
01:21:08.310 --> 01:21:10.520
maybe not January one or February one,

1954
01:21:10.520 --> 01:21:13.680
because it just puts this pressure on it of like,

1955
01:21:13.680 --> 01:21:15.870
well, I have to train,

1956
01:21:15.870 --> 01:21:18.243
I have to do this 'cause it's January.

1957
01:21:19.710 --> 01:21:21.730
Who do I wanna be early summer?

1958
01:21:21.730 --> 01:21:26.730
I wanna run 100K let's say on trails in the early summer.

1959
01:21:26.888 --> 01:21:29.710
Okay, let's just figure out then today,

1960
01:21:29.710 --> 01:21:32.750
maybe this week I'll run 30 minutes three times,

1961
01:21:32.750 --> 01:21:34.130
It's a start.

1962
01:21:34.130 --> 01:21:35.600
It's a start of something.

1963
01:21:35.600 --> 01:21:38.150
And I can at least build some momentum around that.

1964
01:21:38.150 --> 01:21:39.970
Because I think we often overlook

1965
01:21:39.970 --> 01:21:42.433
that we enter a new year already pretty low.

1966
01:21:44.255 --> 01:21:45.410
It's not like you flip a switch

1967
01:21:45.410 --> 01:21:47.431
and everything is great.
The sun's out,

1968
01:21:47.431 --> 01:21:48.609
it's 85 degrees,

1969
01:21:48.609 --> 01:21:50.737
you feel amazing.
You're on an island tanning.

1970
01:21:50.737 --> 01:21:52.303
You're back to your basement-

1971
01:21:52.303 --> 01:21:53.550
ridding the trainer.

1972
01:21:53.550 --> 01:21:55.860
And how do you differentiate

1973
01:21:55.860 --> 01:21:58.540
between kind of indulging in that self care

1974
01:21:58.540 --> 01:22:01.090
of like, oh, I'm tired and I'm just gonna take a break

1975
01:22:01.090 --> 01:22:03.460
and that's okay, I'm not gonna beat myself up

1976
01:22:03.460 --> 01:22:07.892
versus okay dude, come on, you can get out there,

1977
01:22:07.892 --> 01:22:11.683
don't be too soft on this.

1978
01:22:12.690 --> 01:22:15.330
I mean, you wanna set hard dates for that stuff.

1979
01:22:15.330 --> 01:22:19.310
Like if I'm going to take two weeks off and let go

1980
01:22:19.310 --> 01:22:20.240
or three weeks,

1981
01:22:20.240 --> 01:22:23.830
well then I will let go and I will build up the energy.

1982
01:22:23.830 --> 01:22:25.810
See, and I I'll finish this thought,

1983
01:22:25.810 --> 01:22:29.960
I keep running into athletes that don't wanna fully stop

1984
01:22:29.960 --> 01:22:32.220
so that when they need to restart again,

1985
01:22:32.220 --> 01:22:35.300
they don't have that hunger, that energy that bent up like,

1986
01:22:35.300 --> 01:22:37.240
okay, finally, I can get straight again.

1987
01:22:37.240 --> 01:22:40.160
I'm like, stop, stop everything.

1988
01:22:40.160 --> 01:22:42.293
I want you to feel terrible.

1989
01:22:43.750 --> 01:22:45.270
Then kick out and go, okay,

1990
01:22:45.270 --> 01:22:47.370
finally, I can get back into my routine.

1991
01:22:47.370 --> 01:22:49.600
Finally, I feel good and healthy again,

1992
01:22:49.600 --> 01:22:51.720
because let's say not six months,

1993
01:22:51.720 --> 01:22:54.083
but like, let's say two, three, four weeks even

1994
01:22:54.083 --> 01:22:57.040
where you say that to yourself and you go,

1995
01:22:57.040 --> 01:23:00.460
all right, I have to reset completely

1996
01:23:00.460 --> 01:23:04.560
in order to appreciate, to find the joy again,

1997
01:23:04.560 --> 01:23:07.919
that when I I'm like, oh, I missed this so much.

1998
01:23:07.919 --> 01:23:10.800
That is the hardest thing for athletes though,

1999
01:23:10.800 --> 01:23:12.660
to really completely pause.

2000
01:23:12.660 --> 01:23:15.000
And I'm horrible at it.

2001
01:23:15.000 --> 01:23:17.610
Like that's why probably after the Olympics,

2002
01:23:17.610 --> 01:23:19.980
even if I took a week or two off training

2003
01:23:20.900 --> 01:23:23.720
kind of did, my mind was on the next thing

2004
01:23:23.720 --> 01:23:25.740
and it was on it yesterday.

2005
01:23:25.740 --> 01:23:27.390
And I think sometimes

2006
01:23:27.390 --> 01:23:30.180
we have to be completely knocked off our feet

2007
01:23:30.180 --> 01:23:31.250
in order to do that

2008
01:23:31.250 --> 01:23:34.270
and that's such a shame because at that point

2009
01:23:34.270 --> 01:23:38.590
like I only paused really when I had this surgery.

2010
01:23:38.590 --> 01:23:40.350
Like if I'm being really honest,

2011
01:23:40.350 --> 01:23:44.040
that's the only time I've truly paused completely

2012
01:23:44.040 --> 01:23:45.880
as an athlete and let myself reset.

2013
01:23:45.880 --> 01:23:49.910
And that was because I literally could not move off my couch

2014
01:23:49.910 --> 01:23:53.112
and that's a problem.

2015
01:23:53.112 --> 01:23:56.240
And so I think there's two parts to this.

2016
01:23:56.240 --> 01:23:59.400
Like one embracing that,

2017
01:23:59.400 --> 01:24:03.820
we should probably build in like some vocabulary

2018
01:24:03.820 --> 01:24:06.080
where there's a chapter after a big peak

2019
01:24:06.080 --> 01:24:07.860
or before a big buildup,

2020
01:24:07.860 --> 01:24:11.320
that is a part of chasing that dream

2021
01:24:11.320 --> 01:24:13.443
and it's completely necessary.

2022
01:24:15.651 --> 01:24:19.220
I guess I think there's more vocabulary now when someone,

2023
01:24:19.220 --> 01:24:20.880
when a woman gives birth,

2024
01:24:20.880 --> 01:24:24.830
that there's the next trimester after

2025
01:24:24.830 --> 01:24:26.790
is like a more normalized thing.

2026
01:24:26.790 --> 01:24:29.170
So we should probably build into our vocabulary

2027
01:24:29.170 --> 01:24:31.240
that that's actually like normal and safe.

2028
01:24:31.240 --> 01:24:36.240
And it's not an indulgence it's like necessary.

2029
01:24:36.840 --> 01:24:41.840
And then also I think about the butterfly thing.

2030
01:24:42.240 --> 01:24:45.073
Like I think about how when I was,

2031
01:24:46.070 --> 01:24:49.130
look because I still have trouble pausing myself,

2032
01:24:49.130 --> 01:24:52.140
I was kind of forced to pause at one point

2033
01:24:52.140 --> 01:24:55.010
because like a nerve in my back was irritated

2034
01:24:55.010 --> 01:24:57.400
because I didn't pause when I needed to.

2035
01:24:57.400 --> 01:25:01.650
And I felt like I was completely reduced to like nothing.

2036
01:25:01.650 --> 01:25:03.780
And my mentor told me

2037
01:25:03.780 --> 01:25:07.650
that when caterpillar's become butterflies,

2038
01:25:07.650 --> 01:25:10.870
they go into their Chrysalis and they become liquid glop,

2039
01:25:10.870 --> 01:25:14.340
like complete liquid and then they grow into a butterfly.

2040
01:25:14.340 --> 01:25:17.240
They're not just a caterpillar, that sprouts wings.

2041
01:25:17.240 --> 01:25:18.880
And what was so beautiful about that,

2042
01:25:18.880 --> 01:25:22.300
is I think as athletes and as high-achieving people,

2043
01:25:22.300 --> 01:25:23.420
whatever we are,

2044
01:25:23.420 --> 01:25:25.870
when we are reduced to like that pause state,

2045
01:25:25.870 --> 01:25:26.890
whether you're injured

2046
01:25:26.890 --> 01:25:28.850
or whether you choose to take a pause,

2047
01:25:28.850 --> 01:25:30.610
we assume that it's bad

2048
01:25:30.610 --> 01:25:32.820
and we assume that we would like to put back together

2049
01:25:32.820 --> 01:25:35.300
whatever we were and we'll get back to it.

2050
01:25:35.300 --> 01:25:36.760
And what he was basically saying,

2051
01:25:36.760 --> 01:25:38.270
'cause I kept calling myself a hot mess

2052
01:25:38.270 --> 01:25:40.670
and he was like, "No, you're not a hot mess.

2053
01:25:40.670 --> 01:25:42.980
You are glop and you're becoming a butterfly."

2054
01:25:42.980 --> 01:25:47.963
And so I recontextualized it in my mind as that pause,

2055
01:25:48.840 --> 01:25:51.130
which I wish I had imposed upon myself

2056
01:25:51.130 --> 01:25:53.983
instead of mother nature reigning this pain on my back,

2057
01:25:55.060 --> 01:25:57.350
was a natural and good thing.

2058
01:25:57.350 --> 01:25:59.550
And then I started marching around my house and I was like,

2059
01:25:59.550 --> 01:26:02.533
I'm glop, I'm glop and I'm gonna become a butterfly.

2060
01:26:04.086 --> 01:26:06.480
And he was like the difference between humans

2061
01:26:06.480 --> 01:26:08.510
and caterpillar's is that humans

2062
01:26:08.510 --> 01:26:10.430
have to feel the pain of glophood.

2063
01:26:10.430 --> 01:26:12.480
So the pain of pausing,

2064
01:26:12.480 --> 01:26:13.840
that could be really painful for people

2065
01:26:13.840 --> 01:26:16.270
or the pain of the actual pain you're in.

2066
01:26:16.270 --> 01:26:21.100
And caterpillars or humans could choose to try to go back

2067
01:26:21.100 --> 01:26:24.130
to the caterpillar state and just be what they were.

2068
01:26:24.130 --> 01:26:27.010
And I may be like, leaping on what you say

2069
01:26:27.010 --> 01:26:29.280
and assuming that the people you're talking to

2070
01:26:29.280 --> 01:26:32.560
have a lot of trouble being in the Chrysalis and the cocoon,

2071
01:26:32.560 --> 01:26:34.770
and they don't believe in it and they wanna get out of it.

2072
01:26:34.770 --> 01:26:36.180
And they don't think it's helping them.

2073
01:26:36.180 --> 01:26:39.130
They think it is a pause and a hurdle

2074
01:26:39.130 --> 01:26:42.153
rather than a process of moving forward.

2075
01:26:43.010 --> 01:26:45.150
It's hard to see yourself in the mirror

2076
01:26:45.150 --> 01:26:48.490
when you're moving, but when you stop,

2077
01:26:48.490 --> 01:26:49.940
you see yourself in the mirror

2078
01:26:49.940 --> 01:26:52.690
and you might have to confront somethings.

2079
01:26:52.690 --> 01:26:53.583
Terrifying.

2080
01:26:54.417 --> 01:26:55.250
But when you're moving

2081
01:26:55.250 --> 01:26:57.240
you're always charging and moving forward.

2082
01:26:58.410 --> 01:27:00.617
You're all a moving target.
Exactly.

2083
01:27:00.617 --> 01:27:02.760
You said the word safe and that's so real.

2084
01:27:02.760 --> 01:27:05.260
'Cause you're always safe when you're moving forward.

2085
01:27:05.260 --> 01:27:07.099
Which is interesting because-
And it validates you too.

2086
01:27:07.099 --> 01:27:09.580
It's the opposite of what you think.

2087
01:27:09.580 --> 01:27:12.250
Moving forward or back to everything we were saying.

2088
01:27:12.250 --> 01:27:14.221
So we inherently feel unsafe.

2089
01:27:14.221 --> 01:27:16.940
Active denial.
Yeah.

2090
01:27:16.940 --> 01:27:18.040
Active denial.

2091
01:27:18.040 --> 01:27:21.920
I know I had the same exact situation with my heel.

2092
01:27:21.920 --> 01:27:25.410
I mean, I didn't see a single thing until I stopped

2093
01:27:25.410 --> 01:27:27.820
and then reminded me again and I stopped again.

2094
01:27:27.820 --> 01:27:31.330
And so I think it's important to address

2095
01:27:31.330 --> 01:27:34.440
why we feel unsafe when we are pausing.

2096
01:27:34.440 --> 01:27:37.001
I remember like back in the '80s with swimming,

2097
01:27:37.001 --> 01:27:38.377
if you were to take two weeks off,

2098
01:27:38.377 --> 01:27:41.250
it was like, oh my God, two weeks.

2099
01:27:41.250 --> 01:27:43.020
Like if somebody took a season off,

2100
01:27:43.020 --> 01:27:45.043
it would be like, well, they'll, they'll never come back.

2101
01:27:45.043 --> 01:27:47.440
There's no way they could ever come back.

2102
01:27:47.440 --> 01:27:48.943
But it's changing so much.

2103
01:27:50.490 --> 01:27:52.250
This past year specifically,

2104
01:27:52.250 --> 01:27:56.640
because mental health is such a topic on everybody's minds,

2105
01:27:56.640 --> 01:27:58.340
but in the athlete context,

2106
01:27:58.340 --> 01:27:59.860
what we've seen with Simone Biles

2107
01:27:59.860 --> 01:28:01.770
and all these other very high profile athletes

2108
01:28:01.770 --> 01:28:03.340
who are taking a stand for mental health,

2109
01:28:03.340 --> 01:28:05.850
I think it's changing that conversation.

2110
01:28:05.850 --> 01:28:07.980
I think what you said is true Alexi

2111
01:28:07.980 --> 01:28:09.990
like this idea of looking at,

2112
01:28:09.990 --> 01:28:12.750
not only building in rest periods, as fundamental

2113
01:28:12.750 --> 01:28:14.360
as Chris was talking about,

2114
01:28:14.360 --> 01:28:19.010
but looking at them as part of the growth,

2115
01:28:19.010 --> 01:28:23.900
as opposed to an obstacle to the goal.

2116
01:28:23.900 --> 01:28:27.320
And relishing in that, that glop state,

2117
01:28:27.320 --> 01:28:28.153
that's what I call it.

2118
01:28:28.153 --> 01:28:29.720
I want a shirt that says it.

2119
01:28:29.720 --> 01:28:31.130
Glop state.
Just glop.

2120
01:28:31.130 --> 01:28:33.350
I'm in the glop state.
Relish your glop state.

2121
01:28:33.350 --> 01:28:34.909
But here's the thing though,

2122
01:28:34.909 --> 01:28:38.160
with Simone Biles and Naomi Osaka's situation,

2123
01:28:38.160 --> 01:28:43.160
what was so challenging to watch was that they expressed

2124
01:28:44.198 --> 01:28:48.340
like a feeling or a sensation or a fear of a sensation,

2125
01:28:48.340 --> 01:28:50.520
like a fear of pain to come

2126
01:28:50.520 --> 01:28:53.650
and they wanted to protect themselves from that.

2127
01:28:53.650 --> 01:28:56.550
And some people responded really positively,

2128
01:28:56.550 --> 01:28:59.190
but some people said where's the MRI.

2129
01:28:59.190 --> 01:29:00.570
Like where's the proof.

2130
01:29:00.570 --> 01:29:03.870
And that is really, really dangerous

2131
01:29:03.870 --> 01:29:07.560
because there's not always gonna be proof on an MRI

2132
01:29:07.560 --> 01:29:10.940
for a mental health or physical health injury

2133
01:29:10.940 --> 01:29:13.190
and what I've seen over and over again

2134
01:29:13.190 --> 01:29:17.990
and experienced myself is that we need to,

2135
01:29:17.990 --> 01:29:21.330
when there's not proof of that pain,

2136
01:29:21.330 --> 01:29:24.290
simply that we said it needs to be enough.

2137
01:29:24.290 --> 01:29:27.410
Like the word needs to have value in and of itself.

2138
01:29:27.410 --> 01:29:29.620
And I think it's lost its value,

2139
01:29:29.620 --> 01:29:32.800
at least in my observations of the world.

2140
01:29:32.800 --> 01:29:34.630
And I hope that this year,

2141
01:29:34.630 --> 01:29:39.630
people take that word without the MRI and accept it as real,

2142
01:29:39.750 --> 01:29:41.377
because otherwise we're gonna hurt ourselves

2143
01:29:41.377 --> 01:29:46.173
and we're gonna do that active denial endorsed by society.

2144
01:29:47.800 --> 01:29:51.550
And throw into that how long it took that athlete,

2145
01:29:51.550 --> 01:29:56.543
those ladies to even accept themselves sayingit .

2146
01:29:58.110 --> 01:30:00.960
So first you have to accept yourself saying it

2147
01:30:00.960 --> 01:30:05.370
before the waterfall of resistance comes at you

2148
01:30:05.370 --> 01:30:07.490
by others with their expectations for you,

2149
01:30:07.490 --> 01:30:09.950
but your own expectations and you feeling

2150
01:30:09.950 --> 01:30:12.800
like you're letting yourself down, like what we were saying,

2151
01:30:12.800 --> 01:30:17.800
like brow furrowed and focused like, and letting go.

2152
01:30:18.160 --> 01:30:23.160
I mean, that's the world of, again, holding on so tight

2153
01:30:24.100 --> 01:30:26.870
and then all of a sudden recognizing

2154
01:30:26.870 --> 01:30:29.060
you've gone way past the point

2155
01:30:29.060 --> 01:30:31.030
and you should've stopped back there.

2156
01:30:31.030 --> 01:30:33.610
And I think what you're saying, Alexi,

2157
01:30:33.610 --> 01:30:38.210
is also our ability to remain with listening to our gut.

2158
01:30:38.210 --> 01:30:39.780
What you call instinct.

2159
01:30:39.780 --> 01:30:42.243
it's like, we know it,

2160
01:30:42.243 --> 01:30:46.113
we know it's there and just trusting and believing in it

2161
01:30:46.113 --> 01:30:49.100
because it always works out for the better.

2162
01:30:49.100 --> 01:30:52.740
Every time I've trusted my gut in my life,

2163
01:30:52.740 --> 01:30:55.050
as well as when nature comes raining down

2164
01:30:55.050 --> 01:30:57.921
and does this thing afterwards months,

2165
01:30:57.921 --> 01:31:00.549
years after I look back and I say,

2166
01:31:00.549 --> 01:31:02.547
that was the best thing that could have happened to me.

2167
01:31:02.547 --> 01:31:04.480
But what if you're like a college girl

2168
01:31:04.480 --> 01:31:06.667
trying to tell your coach,

2169
01:31:06.667 --> 01:31:09.280
you're fighting for a spot on the cross country team

2170
01:31:09.280 --> 01:31:11.710
and you're trying to tell them my gut feels like

2171
01:31:11.710 --> 01:31:14.420
this is not great for me or I need a pause,

2172
01:31:14.420 --> 01:31:18.430
I think we need to equip people with vocabulary

2173
01:31:18.430 --> 01:31:21.120
that will translate to audiences

2174
01:31:21.120 --> 01:31:23.210
that might not be ready to hear it.

2175
01:31:23.210 --> 01:31:26.860
And so I think part of what we're saying here

2176
01:31:26.860 --> 01:31:28.810
needs to come with like some guidance

2177
01:31:28.810 --> 01:31:32.150
on like what words will possibly resonate

2178
01:31:32.150 --> 01:31:33.980
with the people that need to hear it

2179
01:31:33.980 --> 01:31:36.010
who don't understand it yet.

2180
01:31:36.010 --> 01:31:37.320
I know that you're saying

2181
01:31:37.320 --> 01:31:38.810
that we need to understand it ourselves,

2182
01:31:38.810 --> 01:31:42.240
but I just think about like a scared 20 year old girl

2183
01:31:42.240 --> 01:31:45.780
who thinks running this race might not be a good idea,

2184
01:31:45.780 --> 01:31:48.990
but doesn't know how to advocate.

2185
01:31:48.990 --> 01:31:51.083
Or if she raises her hand and says that,

2186
01:31:51.083 --> 01:31:54.760
that she'll just get cut.
Exactly.

2187
01:31:54.760 --> 01:31:57.630
I think it just goes back to coaches

2188
01:31:57.630 --> 01:32:00.060
and whomever looking within themselves

2189
01:32:00.060 --> 01:32:04.540
and actually understanding what it means for themselves.

2190
01:32:04.540 --> 01:32:08.650
And I'm just speaking from a perspective of,

2191
01:32:08.650 --> 01:32:11.890
I think there's resistance maybe

2192
01:32:11.890 --> 01:32:14.310
to hearing that an athlete isn't okay.

2193
01:32:14.310 --> 01:32:16.860
It's like, what do you mean you're not okay.

2194
01:32:16.860 --> 01:32:18.290
No, no, you have to be okay.

2195
01:32:18.290 --> 01:32:20.050
Like you have to perform.

2196
01:32:20.050 --> 01:32:23.650
And so maybe it's just sitting as a coach or as a mentor,

2197
01:32:23.650 --> 01:32:24.750
as a guide or whomever.

2198
01:32:24.750 --> 01:32:27.120
And to your point earlier, as well,

2199
01:32:27.120 --> 01:32:28.680
looking within yourself and saying,

2200
01:32:28.680 --> 01:32:29.600
what have I been through

2201
01:32:29.600 --> 01:32:32.780
that I can then pass on to my athletes.

2202
01:32:32.780 --> 01:32:35.400
So I think it takes everybody doing the introspection,

2203
01:32:35.400 --> 01:32:38.720
everybody becoming aware of their own internal experience

2204
01:32:38.720 --> 01:32:41.033
to then accept what someone's telling you.

2205
01:32:41.033 --> 01:32:43.050
What an athlete is telling you.

2206
01:32:43.050 --> 01:32:44.220
But I don't know that those coaches

2207
01:32:44.220 --> 01:32:46.360
are listening to what our conversation right now.

2208
01:32:46.360 --> 01:32:47.390
I don't know that people,

2209
01:32:47.390 --> 01:32:48.653
I'm just being honest

2210
01:32:48.653 --> 01:32:49.880
that I don't know if the people who really need to hear it

2211
01:32:49.880 --> 01:32:51.780
are actually listening to this conversation.

2212
01:32:51.780 --> 01:32:54.630
And I think what I would say to this young girl

2213
01:32:54.630 --> 01:32:57.253
who is our fictitious person in front of us now,

2214
01:32:58.580 --> 01:33:02.930
is that like you returned to the common goal

2215
01:33:02.930 --> 01:33:04.600
'cause I think all the people hearing it

2216
01:33:04.600 --> 01:33:07.020
and need to know is that we all want the same thing.

2217
01:33:07.020 --> 01:33:10.180
Like we all want to win the cross country championships.

2218
01:33:10.180 --> 01:33:11.013
You know what I mean?

2219
01:33:11.013 --> 01:33:11.870
We all want that

2220
01:33:11.870 --> 01:33:13.960
and I think the best thing to get there

2221
01:33:13.960 --> 01:33:16.500
is to take this race off or whatever.

2222
01:33:16.500 --> 01:33:19.050
And I think part of this conversation

2223
01:33:19.050 --> 01:33:21.220
is when we're trying to rally people

2224
01:33:21.220 --> 01:33:22.900
onto our new year's goals,

2225
01:33:22.900 --> 01:33:25.620
'cause you don't wanna do it despite everybody,

2226
01:33:25.620 --> 01:33:27.990
it's nice to have people on your same page.

2227
01:33:27.990 --> 01:33:30.260
I think it's important to include them

2228
01:33:30.260 --> 01:33:32.840
as if every decision you're making for yourself

2229
01:33:32.840 --> 01:33:36.050
is also best for the team

2230
01:33:36.050 --> 01:33:38.840
or whatever that team is, a family, a coach.

2231
01:33:38.840 --> 01:33:41.470
And the only reason I'm saying this

2232
01:33:41.470 --> 01:33:45.040
is 'cause I feel like a lot of what I hear is like,

2233
01:33:45.040 --> 01:33:46.550
yes, I want all that for myself

2234
01:33:46.550 --> 01:33:48.130
But there's so many barriers in my way.

2235
01:33:48.130 --> 01:33:52.313
And trying to help people do it despite the barriers,

2236
01:33:53.790 --> 01:33:56.173
which might be coaches or something else.

2237
01:33:56.173 --> 01:33:58.057
I mean, I have two thoughts on that one,

2238
01:33:58.057 --> 01:34:00.230
and too often it's about the coaches

2239
01:34:00.230 --> 01:34:02.990
and they think it's about me versus the athlete.

2240
01:34:02.990 --> 01:34:05.300
And it's the accolades the coaches are getting,

2241
01:34:05.300 --> 01:34:07.070
which then goes up to athletic director,

2242
01:34:07.070 --> 01:34:09.460
which then goes up to the (indistinct), right.

2243
01:34:09.460 --> 01:34:12.960
So, and it's never about them, it's about the athlete.

2244
01:34:12.960 --> 01:34:15.504
And so that's a whole culture

2245
01:34:15.504 --> 01:34:18.270
that I don't think we're gonna solve right now.

2246
01:34:18.270 --> 01:34:20.600
But whenever I see that, I'm always like,

2247
01:34:20.600 --> 01:34:22.670
it's a coach making it about themselves

2248
01:34:22.670 --> 01:34:27.000
because they look good in that arena or at that race

2249
01:34:27.000 --> 01:34:29.700
or in that national championship when they're winning.

2250
01:34:30.780 --> 01:34:34.070
If you're not listening to your athletes,

2251
01:34:34.070 --> 01:34:35.260
so that's the one side.

2252
01:34:35.260 --> 01:34:38.130
But it's also what you were just saying with new years,

2253
01:34:38.130 --> 01:34:40.297
it's also back to who you want to be

2254
01:34:40.297 --> 01:34:44.683
and when I close my eyes and I visualize who I want to be,

2255
01:34:44.683 --> 01:34:47.247
that means at that finish line,

2256
01:34:47.247 --> 01:34:50.010
my family's there and they're supporting me

2257
01:34:50.010 --> 01:34:51.650
and they don't think I'm a jerk

2258
01:34:51.650 --> 01:34:55.000
because of the last 12 weeks or 16 weeks of training.

2259
01:34:55.000 --> 01:34:56.520
They're actually like, you know what?

2260
01:34:56.520 --> 01:35:00.220
He did all training and he still was present for us.

2261
01:35:00.220 --> 01:35:01.956
And he was still,

2262
01:35:01.956 --> 01:35:04.620
I still was an effective person at work

2263
01:35:04.620 --> 01:35:06.220
and all those things too often.

2264
01:35:06.220 --> 01:35:10.550
Again, when we create these alter or split personalities,

2265
01:35:10.550 --> 01:35:14.020
we push the others away and we don't realize

2266
01:35:14.020 --> 01:35:16.910
how they're being affected by us,

2267
01:35:16.910 --> 01:35:20.480
on our self care, growth, fitness movement.

2268
01:35:20.480 --> 01:35:23.670
And we need to think about that prior,

2269
01:35:23.670 --> 01:35:26.270
especially if it's a curious goal

2270
01:35:26.270 --> 01:35:28.860
that hasn't been part of our repertoire in the past

2271
01:35:28.860 --> 01:35:30.170
where it's like, guess what?

2272
01:35:30.170 --> 01:35:31.830
I wanna try this thing.

2273
01:35:31.830 --> 01:35:33.960
It's gonna take a lot of training hours

2274
01:35:33.960 --> 01:35:37.850
and it's gonna be running in the mountains for 30 hours,

2275
01:35:37.850 --> 01:35:40.000
and what do you guys think?

2276
01:35:40.000 --> 01:35:41.600
And they're gonna be like, what?

2277
01:35:42.630 --> 01:35:43.560
How many weeks?

2278
01:35:43.560 --> 01:35:45.970
But it's okay, I'm thinking about that.

2279
01:35:45.970 --> 01:35:47.517
And this is what I wanna do next summer.

2280
01:35:47.517 --> 01:35:49.120
And how can we include this?

2281
01:35:49.120 --> 01:35:50.820
And how can we all work on this together,

2282
01:35:50.820 --> 01:35:53.060
and these are the values, again,

2283
01:35:53.060 --> 01:35:55.620
the boundaries I'm gonna create myself for myself,

2284
01:35:55.620 --> 01:35:59.500
the lane I'm gonna stay in and I'm not gonna compromise

2285
01:35:59.500 --> 01:36:02.630
and cross over when push comes to shove.

2286
01:36:02.630 --> 01:36:05.620
'Cause I talked to you guys here on January 6th

2287
01:36:05.620 --> 01:36:07.770
or January 20th and I said,

2288
01:36:07.770 --> 01:36:10.820
all right, I come, I'm not gonna miss a soccer game.

2289
01:36:10.820 --> 01:36:13.210
I'm not gonna turn down those projects to work

2290
01:36:13.210 --> 01:36:14.700
just because I wanna do,

2291
01:36:14.700 --> 01:36:16.097
I'm gonna do it despite that

2292
01:36:16.097 --> 01:36:18.390
and it's back to maximizing

2293
01:36:18.390 --> 01:36:20.430
the limited training time we have,

2294
01:36:20.430 --> 01:36:24.930
staying with who we want to be and that's the challenge.

2295
01:36:24.930 --> 01:36:27.830
We come from a professional athlete background.

2296
01:36:27.830 --> 01:36:30.730
But for all those people who have a job who have a family,

2297
01:36:31.720 --> 01:36:34.050
a soccer game, who have church, who have community,

2298
01:36:34.050 --> 01:36:37.560
it's like, well, come on, Chris, how am I gonna fit this in?

2299
01:36:37.560 --> 01:36:39.940
Well, that's what makes you special.

2300
01:36:39.940 --> 01:36:42.990
That's what makes this endeavor so amazing,

2301
01:36:42.990 --> 01:36:47.480
is that despite at all, you found those 45 minutes a day.

2302
01:36:47.480 --> 01:36:49.250
And you might say 40 minutes a day,

2303
01:36:49.250 --> 01:36:51.150
that's not enough to do this stuff.

2304
01:36:51.150 --> 01:36:51.983
Well guess what?

2305
01:36:51.983 --> 01:36:54.227
45 minutes a day for many months,

2306
01:36:54.227 --> 01:36:58.010
it'll get you pretty close to doing what you can.

2307
01:36:58.010 --> 01:37:00.400
It might not be to the best of your potential.

2308
01:37:00.400 --> 01:37:05.400
You might think you can run Boston in 345 or 245,

2309
01:37:06.050 --> 01:37:08.120
and you might only do 415.

2310
01:37:08.120 --> 01:37:08.953
But guess what?

2311
01:37:08.953 --> 01:37:11.120
You didn't compromise your values along the way,

2312
01:37:11.120 --> 01:37:13.300
your family's there cheering on supporting you.

2313
01:37:13.300 --> 01:37:15.270
And they actually like that was amazing.

2314
01:37:15.270 --> 01:37:18.240
Let's do it again and try for 345 versus,

2315
01:37:18.240 --> 01:37:21.120
that 345 attempt was awful.

2316
01:37:21.120 --> 01:37:24.590
So maybe potential then is not an isolated thing

2317
01:37:24.590 --> 01:37:28.200
that has only to do with your actual body and genes,

2318
01:37:28.200 --> 01:37:31.530
potential is seeing yourself as a whole person

2319
01:37:31.530 --> 01:37:33.470
and seeing where you can go

2320
01:37:33.470 --> 01:37:37.160
given all that you have chosen in your life,

2321
01:37:37.160 --> 01:37:40.300
or that has been put on you in your life,

2322
01:37:40.300 --> 01:37:42.800
like your circumstances that you can't choose

2323
01:37:42.800 --> 01:37:44.540
and that you might not have chosen.

2324
01:37:44.540 --> 01:37:46.540
And so maybe when we think about potential,

2325
01:37:46.540 --> 01:37:48.810
